---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Why does traverse-mcp from crates.io fail with catalog_load_failed?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'traverse-mcp 0.15.0 installed with cargo install loads the expedition example bundle at startup, even in Mode A, and it looks for it at a path compiled in from CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR. That path only exists inside a traverse repo checkout, so the installed binary exits with catalog_load_failed before it answers describe_server. Workarounds: run it from a checkout at tag v0.15.0 (cargo run -p traverse-mcp -- stdio), or stage the 13 expedition fixture files from v0.15.0 at the path the error prints, which is what the October 23 weekly demo does. The fix is merged on main (traverse#1725, closing traverse#1720, October 27, 2026): Mode A no longer loads the bundle and contributor mode takes an explicit --bundle flag. It is released in Traverse v0.16.0 (November 2, 2026): the easiest fix is the prebuilt traverse-mcp-v0.16.0 binary, which needs no Rust toolchain. 0.15.0 still needs a workaround.',
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/why-does-traverse-mcp-fail-with-registry-metadata-cache-invalid.html', label: 'Why does traverse-mcp fail with registry_metadata_cache_invalid?' },
  { href: '/questions/is-the-agent-mcp-demo-the-same-as-traverse-mcp.html', label: 'Is the agent MCP demo the same as traverse-mcp?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-does-the-mcp-server-work.html', label: 'How does the Traverse MCP server work?' },
  { href: '/blog/hash-converges.html', label: 'Weekly demo: the main-built traverse-mcp runs from an empty directory' },
  { href: '/blog/agent-mcp-real-server.html', label: 'Weekly demo: the agent gets the real MCP server' },
  { href: '/agents.html', label: 'For AI agents (traverse-mcp)' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Why does traverse-mcp from crates.io fail with catalog_load_failed?"
  description="traverse-mcp 0.15.0 from cargo install looks for the expedition example bundle at a compiled-in repo path, so it can't start outside a traverse checkout. Two workarounds, and the fix released in v0.16.0 (traverse#1720), with prebuilt binaries."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/why-does-traverse-mcp-fail-with-catalog-load-failed.html"
  category="AI and MCP"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> it's a packaging bug in <code>traverse-mcp</code> 0.15.0, not your setup. The installed binary goes looking for the expedition example bundle at a path that only exists inside a traverse repo checkout. It does this before it checks whether you asked for Mode A, so it fails even with <code>--cache</code>. The fix is merged on <code>main</code> (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1725">#1725</a>, closing <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1720">traverse#1720</a>) and released in Traverse v0.16.0 on November 2, 2026.</p>

  <h2>Update, November 2, 2026: fixed in v0.16.0, with prebuilt binaries</h2>
  <p>Skip <code>cargo install</code>. <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/traverse-mcp-v0.16.0"><code>traverse-mcp-v0.16.0</code></a> ships checksum-pinned <code>traverse-mcp</code> binaries for Linux x64, Linux arm64, macOS arm64 and Windows x64, and Mode A is three commands with no Rust toolchain:</p>
  <pre><code>curl -L https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/download/traverse-mcp-v0.16.0/traverse-mcp-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz | tar -xz
./traverse-mcp sync --out synced-state.json
./traverse-mcp prepare-cache --synced-state synced-state.json --cache traverse-cache --ref core/core.authorize@^1</code></pre>
  <p>We ran the Linux x64 binary from an empty directory on November 2: <code>--version</code> reports <code>0.16.0</code>, <code>sync</code> wrote registry <code>index-v412</code> (232 records), <code>prepare-cache</code> verified <code>core.authorize@1.2.0</code>, and <code>describe_server</code> answered from the verified cache. The 0.16.0 crates weren't on crates.io yet when we checked; the rest of this page is about the 0.15.0 crate.</p>

  <h2>What you see</h2>
  <p>You install the published crate and start the stdio server:</p>
  <pre><code>cargo install traverse-mcp --version 0.15.0 --locked
echo '{"{"}"command":"describe_server"{"}"}' | traverse-mcp stdio --cache ./cache</code></pre>
  <p>and it exits with:</p>
  <pre><code>traverse-mcp stdio server failed: StdioServerFailure {"{"} code: "catalog_load_failed",
  message: "Failed to load expedition registry bundle
  ~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-…/traverse-mcp-0.15.0/../../examples/expedition/registry-bundle/manifest.json:
  … No such file or directory" {"}"}</code></pre>

  <h2>Why it happens</h2>
  <p>On startup the server loads the canonical execution context and discovery catalog from <code>examples/expedition/registry-bundle/manifest.json</code>, relative to a repo root that is computed at compile time from <code>CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR</code>. Inside the monorepo that resolves to the real example. In a crates.io install it resolves to a folder next to Cargo's registry source cache, which is empty. Mode A (Spec 119) isn't supposed to fall back to the expedition example at all, but startup still requires it.</p>

  <h2>Workarounds on v0.15.0</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Run it from a checkout.</strong> Clone <code>traverse</code>, check out tag <code>v0.15.0</code>, and use <code>cargo run -p traverse-mcp -- stdio</code>. That is the path the <a href="/docs/guides/mcp-setup.html">MCP setup guide</a> uses, and the compiled-in path exists there.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage the fixture files.</strong> Copy the 13 files the server reads (the expedition registry bundle manifest, six capability contracts, five event contracts, one workflow) from tag <code>v0.15.0</code> to the path the error prints, under <code>$CARGO_HOME/registry/src/</code>. The October 23 weekly demo does exactly this with a small script before it starts Mode A. After that, the server comes up in <code>verified_public</code> mode and executes correctly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Both are workarounds. The second one writes into Cargo's cache directory, so treat it as a demo trick, not something to ship.</p>

  <h2>What the fix looks like</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1725">#1725</a> merged on <code>main</code> on October 27, 2026. Packaged <code>traverse-mcp stdio</code> now picks verified-cache Mode A before it loads any contributor bundle, so Mode A never touches the expedition example. Contributor mode needs an explicit <code>--bundle &lt;manifest-path&gt;</code> instead of a path derived from <code>CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR</code>, and a missing bundle gives a stable error. It shipped in v0.16.0; on 0.15.0 the workarounds above are still what you need.</p>
  <p>traverse#1720 is closed and the fix is released. The <a href="/blog/hash-converges.html">October 30 weekly demo</a> runs <code>traverse-mcp</code> built from <code>main</code> (<code>70f3ee7</code>, Rust 1.95.0) from an empty directory, with no staged fixtures.</p>

  <h2>Related: prebuilt binaries shipped in v0.16.0</h2>
  <p>On 0.15.0 you hit this because <code>cargo install</code> was the only way to get the binary. That changed with v0.16.0: <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/traverse-mcp-v0.16.0"><code>traverse-mcp-v0.16.0</code></a> carries checksum-pinned binaries for four targets with <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and build provenance (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1653">#1653</a>), and <code>traverse-mcp sync</code> writes the synced-state for you (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1721">traverse#1721</a>, closed). An outside developer doesn't need a Rust toolchain for Mode A.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
